@page "/runner"
@inject NavigationManager Navigation
<PageTitle>Run your client — Retry Assurance</PageTitle><h1>Run your client</h1><p>The client runs on your computer or in your build. These examples require the .NET 10 runtime.</p>
<section class="panel"><h2>1. Get a project key</h2><p><a href="/projects/new">Create a project</a>, then create a runner key on its page.</p><p>In PowerShell 7, enter it privately:</p><pre>$env:RA_PROJECT_TOKEN = Read-Host -MaskInput 'Project runner key'</pre><p class="quiet">For a build pipeline, use its secret store. Do not use production credentials or customer data.</p></section>
<section class="panel"><h2>2. Download the examples</h2><p><a class="button" href="https://github.com/muhammetozeski/RetryAssurance/releases/tag/v1.0.0" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Download example clients</a></p><p>Extract the ZIP to a writable folder and open RetryAssurance.Examples.NET10. Run these commands from that folder.</p><details><summary>Build from source instead</summary><p>Clone the <a href="https://github.com/muhammetozeski/RetryAssurance" target="_blank" rel="noopener">repository</a> and use the .NET 10 SDK:</p><pre>dotnet build examples/BrokenClient/BrokenClient.csproj -c Release
dotnet build examples/CorrectClient/CorrectClient.csproj -c Release</pre><p>For a source build, replace the DLL path below with its examples/NAME/bin/Release/net10.0/NAME.dll path.</p></details></section>
<section class="panel"><h2>3. Try the changing-key example</h2><pre>@BrokenCommand</pre><p>Its retry uses a new key and creates two jobs. Expected result: fail, exit code 1.</p><h2>4. Try the stable-key example</h2><pre>@CorrectCommand</pre><p>Its retry keeps the same key and reuses one job. Expected result: pass, exit code 0.</p><p>Each command prints a link to its result.</p></section>
<section class="panel"><h2>5. Compare a new run</h2><p>Use the first run's ID as the baseline:</p><pre>@BaselineCommand</pre><p>For your own client, add the same baseline and revision options to your own command. Running CorrectClient is an example, not a retest of your client.</p><details><summary>Files and exit codes</summary><p>Add <code>--json PRIVATE_OUTPUT_FILE</code> to save a result with local timing observations. Exit codes: 0 pass, 1 fail, 2 incomplete or an action error.</p><p>Logs are saved beside the extracted client in AppCache/Logs. Exported evidence includes your supplied labels and notes; keep it private.</p></details></section>
<section id="own-client" class="panel"><h2>Connect your own client</h2><p>Give your client <code>OperationContext.HttpClient</code>, invoke its create method once and map its result to <code>ClientObservation</code>. Use dummy credentials and test input.</p><p>The current check supports JSON POST jobs, a stable Idempotency-Key and a timeout below three seconds. It does not support a client that uses its own separate transport, polling routes or uploads.</p><a href="https://github.com/muhammetozeski/RetryAssurance/blob/main/examples/ExampleClients/SyntheticJobClient.cs" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Adapter example</a></section>
@code{
string Origin=>new Uri(Navigation.BaseUri).GetLeftPart(UriPartial.Authority);
string BrokenCommand=>$"dotnet BrokenClient/BrokenClient.dll --server {Origin} --commit YOUR_COMMIT";
string CorrectCommand=>$"dotnet CorrectClient/CorrectClient.dll --server {Origin} --commit YOUR_COMMIT";
string BaselineCommand=>$"dotnet CorrectClient/CorrectClient.dll --server {Origin} --commit YOUR_NEW_COMMIT --baseline BASELINE_RUN_ID --revision 1";
}
